bundle-bump(1) -- Update gems in the Gemfile to their latest versions
====================================================================

## SYNOPSIS

`bundle bump` <*patterns> [--group=GROUP]
                         [--all]
                         [--local]
                         [--quiet]
                         [--gemfile=GEMFILE]
                         [--patch|--minor|--major]
                         [--pre]
                         [--strict]
                         [--interactive]
                         [--exact|--tilde|--gte|--lte]
                         [--version=VERSION]
                         [--cooldown=NUMBER]

## DESCRIPTION

`bundle bump` updates the version requirements of the gems matching the given
patterns in the [`Gemfile(5)`](https://guides.rubygems.org/gemfile/) to their newest versions,
and then installs them. It is the counterpart to
[bundle update(1)](bundle-update.1.html), which only updates `Gemfile.lock`
within the requirements already declared in the `Gemfile`.

Patterns support globs matched against the gems declared in the `Gemfile`.
Quote the pattern to stop your shell from expanding it. A specific version
or requirement can be requested with `gem@version`, or with `--version`:

* `bundle bump rails` updates the `rails` requirement to the latest version.
* `bundle bump rails@8.0.0` sets the `rails` requirement to exactly `8.0.0`.
* `bundle bump 'rake_tasks@> 5.0.0, < 5.1.1'` sets a compound requirement.
* `bundle bump rake_tasks --version='> 5.0.0, < 5.1.1'` does the same for every
  matched gem.
* `bundle bump "rake*"` updates every declared gem whose name starts with `rake`.

At least one pattern is required: unlike `bundle update --all`, `bundle bump`
never rewrites the whole `Gemfile` unprompted. To match every declared gem,
quote a `*` glob (`bundle bump "*"`) or pass `--all`.

The newest version is the newest one that resolves: it has to support the
current Ruby and RubyGems, work with the rest of the bundle, and be outside
the cooldown window, so a gem is never moved to a version that `bundle install`
would then reject. A version below the locked one is never chosen.

Only gems declared directly in the `Gemfile` can be updated. Transitive
dependencies are resolved as usual during install. Gems from `git` or `path`
sources, and gems that are not part of the bundle for the current platform
(e.g. platform-restricted gems like `tzinfo-data`), are skipped with a
warning, since their versions are not managed through version requirements.

## OPTIONS

* `--gemfile=GEMFILE`:
  Use the specified gemfile instead of [`Gemfile(5)`](https://guides.rubygems.org/gemfile/).

* `--group=<list>`, `-g=<list>`:
  Only update the gems in the specified group.

* `--all`:
  Update every gem declared in the `Gemfile`. Cannot be combined with
  patterns or `--group`.

* `--local`:
  Do not attempt to fetch gems remotely and use the gem cache instead.

* `--quiet`:
  Only output warnings and errors.

* `--patch`:
  Update only to the newest patch version, for example `1.0.1` to `1.0.2`.

* `--minor`:
  Update only to the newest minor version, for example `1.0.1` to `1.1.0`.

* `--major`:
  Update to the newest version, even across major versions (default).

* `--pre`:
  Always choose the highest allowed version, regardless of prerelease status.

* `--strict`:
  Do not allow any gem to be updated past latest `--patch` | `--minor` |
  `--major`. `--patch` and `--minor` already keep the selected gems inside
  their level; `--strict` extends that to the rest of the resolution, so a
  transitive dependency cannot move past the level either.

* `--interactive`, `-i`:
  Confirm each gem update individually.

* `--exact`:
  Use an exact version requirement when rewriting the Gemfile.

* `--tilde`:
  Use a pessimistic version requirement when rewriting the Gemfile.

* `--gte`:
  Use an optimistic version requirement when rewriting the Gemfile.

* `--lte`:
  Use a ceiling version requirement when rewriting the Gemfile.

* `--version=VERSION`, `-v=VERSION`:
  Use the given requirement(s) when rewriting the Gemfile. Accepts a single
  version or requirement (`8.0.0`, `>= 8.0`) as well as a comma separated
  list (`> 5.0.0, < 5.1.1`). Cannot be combined with `gem@version` pins,
  with `--all`, or with `--exact`, `--tilde`, `--gte` or `--lte`.

* `--cooldown=<number>`:
  Only consider gem versions published at least <number> days ago when
  resolving. Pass `0` to disable cooldown for this run, overriding every
  other cooldown setting, RubyGems' own included. See `cooldown` in
  bundle-config(1).

## REWRITING REQUIREMENTS

By default, `bundle bump` keeps the operator already declared in the `Gemfile`:

* `~> 7.0` becomes `~> 8.0`, `>= 7.0` (including `>= 0`) becomes `>= 8.0`, and
  `= 7.0` becomes `= 8.0`.
* A bare version with no operator stays bare: `gem "rails", "8.0.0"` becomes
  `gem "rails", "8.0.1"`.
* A declaration with no version gets a `~>` requirement.
* `>` becomes `>=`, with a warning, since `> VERSION` would exclude the
  version it moves to.
* `<`, `<=` and `!=` become `~>`, with a warning, since a ceiling cannot be
  kept when moving to a newer version.
* A declaration with several requirements, like `">= 7.0", "< 8.0"`, keeps
  the operator of the first one and drops the others, with a warning.

Only the requirement strings are replaced: the options, comments and layout of
the declaration are kept, in the `Gemfile` and in any file it loads with
`eval_gemfile`. Every declaration of the gem is rewritten, for example in both
branches of an `if`. A declaration without a literal name (`gem name`) or
with its requirements in an array (`gem "rails", ["~> 7.0"]`) cannot be
rewritten, and `bundle bump` stops with an error instead.

`--patch` and `--minor` only move a gem within its current level, and leave it
alone when nothing newer exists at that level, instead of promoting it to the
next level: `bundle bump rails --patch` never crosses a minor or major version.
Pass `--strict` to keep the rest of the resolution inside the level as well,
matching `bundle update`.

One of `--exact`, `--tilde`, `--gte` or `--lte` overrides this,
using the latest resolved version:

* `--exact` writes `= VERSION`.
* `--tilde` writes `~> VERSION`.
* `--gte` writes `>= VERSION`.
* `--lte` writes `<= VERSION`.

A `gem@version` pin or `--version` always writes the given requirement(s)
verbatim: a single version as `= VERSION`, anything else (including comma
separated compounds like `> 5.0.0, < 5.1.1`) as declared. A pin can name a
prerelease version without passing `--pre`. Giving the same gem two different
pins, for example through two patterns, is an error.

If the install fails, the `Gemfile` and `Gemfile.lock` are restored.

If a gem is already at the newest version, the `Gemfile` is left alone —
unless an operator flag is given, in which case the requirement is still
rewritten to match the locked version.

## COMPARISON WITH YARN UP

`bundle bump` follows [`yarn up`](https://yarnpkg.com/cli/up):

* `yarn up lodash` is `bundle bump rails`.
* `yarn up lodash@1.2.3` is `bundle bump rails@8.0.0`.
* `yarn up "*jest*"` is `bundle bump "*rake*"`.
* `yarn up -i lodash` is `bundle bump -i rails`.
* `yarn up -E` and `-T` (exact and tilde) are `--exact` and `--tilde`. There
  is no equivalent of `-C` (caret), since `^` is not a valid RubyGems
  requirement operator; `--gte` writes an open-ended `>=` requirement instead.
* `yarn up -R` has no equivalent: transitive dependencies are updated with
  bundle-update(1) or `bundle lock --update`.

## EXAMPLES

1. Update the `rails` requirement to the latest available version:

   `bundle bump rails`

2. Pin `rails` to exactly `8.0.0`:

   `bundle bump rails@8.0.0`

3. Update every declared gem whose name starts with `rake`, confirming each:

   `bundle bump "rake*" --interactive`

